<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                    xmlns:views="clr-namespace:WaveLinkBackup.App.Views">

    <!--
      README, "Space, shape, motion": 140ms hover, 220ms state change, easing cubic-bezier(.2,0,0,1),
      no bounce, no slide, and hover changes colour OR position, never both.

      This file holds the one shared piece - the curve. The two durations are written literally at
      each storyboard rather than kept here as resources, because a Duration cannot be declared as
      a XAML resource without a type-converter dance that reads worse than the two literals it
      replaces; MotionTests pins that every animation in the app uses one of them and this easing,
      which is the property that actually matters.

      Every animation in this app targets Opacity or MaxHeight. That is not a coincidence and it is
      not laziness: WPF cannot animate a Background between two brushes held as theme RESOURCES -
      a resource brush is frozen, and animating its Color throws. So a hover is a separate layer
      fading in over the surface rather than the surface changing colour, which has the useful side
      effect of surviving a live theme swap untouched.
    -->
    <views:CubicBezierEase x:Key="WlStandardEase" X1="0.2" Y1="0" X2="0" Y2="1" />

</ResourceDictionary>
